# acad-mechanical domain traps

These are the mistakes mechanical drafters make when they let a beginner —
human or AI — drive the keyboard. They sit on top of the universal domain
rule 35.

## 1. Linetype encodes the visibility class — three layers, not one

Mechanical drawings split every edge into one of three visibility classes
and EACH gets its own layer + linetype:

| edge class       | layer        | linetype | colour |
| ---------------- | ------------ | -------- | ------ |
| visible          | `ME-VISIBLE` | Continuous | 7    |
| hidden           | `ME-HIDDEN`  | HIDDEN     | 8    |
| centre / axis    | `ME-CENTER`  | CENTER     | 4    |

Drafting a hidden edge as a `Continuous` line on `ME-VISIBLE` is the #1 cause
of a "looks fine, fails inspection" drawing. Use `draw_hidden_edge` /
`draw_centerline` rather than `acad-geometry2d.draw_line` directly when the
edge has semantic meaning.

## 2. Centrelines have a SHAPE, not just a linetype

A round-feature centreline is two perpendicular short dashes that EXTEND
beyond the feature outline by ~3-5 mm (ISO 128). For a circular hole,
`draw_centerline_cross` produces both axes at once and sizes the extension
based on the feature radius — agents that draw two `draw_centerline` calls
typically forget the extension and the result looks like a `+` glued to the
circle.

## 3. Section cutting plane line ≠ regular polyline

A section cut (ISO 128 type H) is:
- a **thick** chained line (lineweight 0.70 mm by default) on `ME-SECTION`;
- with an arrow head at each end pointing in the viewing direction;
- with a sectioned-view label at each end (`A`, `A-A`, `B-B`, …).

`draw_section_cut_line` MUST emit all three things together. Returning only
the polyline forces the agent to chase the two arrows + two labels by hand.
The sectioned hatch (on `ME-HATCH`) is a SEPARATE call — sections live in
their OWN view, not on top of the cutting plane.

## 4. Holes have a STANDARD plan-view representation

The plan view (top view, looking down the hole axis) of every hole is a
**circle plus a centreline crosshair**. The side view (looking perpendicular
to the hole axis) adds:

- through hole         → 2 hidden vertical lines + the centreline;
- counterbore          → 2 hidden vertical lines (deep) + 2 hidden vertical
                         lines (shallow, wider) + the centreline;
- countersink          → 2 hidden lines that flare out at 82°/90°/120°;
- blind hole           → 2 hidden vertical lines + a 118° hidden V at the
                         bottom (drill point);
- threaded (tapped)    → an OUTER circle on `ME-VISIBLE` (major Ø) and an
                         INNER 3/4 arc on `ME-THREAD` (minor Ø, NOT a full
                         circle — the gap shows on the right or top
                         depending on draftsman convention).

`draw_through_hole`, `draw_counterbore_hole`, `draw_threaded_hole` cover the
plan view. Side-view variants land in Phase 7.

## 4a. Threaded hole: the "missing arc" is non-negotiable

The minor-diameter circle on a threaded hole is canonically drawn as a
3/4 arc from 0° to 270° (or whatever 270° span the office prefers) — this is
how every mechanical CAD package since Pro/E has shipped it. A FULL inner
circle is **wrong** even though it looks symmetric.

Enforcement: `acad-mechanical.draw_threaded_hole` always produces an Arc
(never a Circle) on `ME-THREAD`. A validator `mech.threads.minor-arc-not-full-circle`
will land once the validator engine grows an `entity_class_equals` check
primitive — until then the convention is enforced by the draw tool, not by
post-hoc validation. Track in CHANGELOG when the primitive ships.

## 5. Bolt-head top view = regular hexagon flat-to-flat

Looking down a hex-head bolt's axis you see a regular hexagon with two flats
parallel to the X axis (default). The flat-to-flat distance is 1.5 ×
nominal diameter for ISO standard heads (M10 → 15 mm flat-to-flat → ~17.3 mm
across-corners). `draw_bolt_head_top_view` MUST take a `flatToFlatMm`
argument, NOT a "size" string — agents pass nominal diameters and the tool
converts.

## 6. Revision triangles are equilateral and number-tagged

Every revision marker on a mechanical drawing is a **filled equilateral
triangle** with the revision letter / number inside. The triangle pointer
sits at the affected feature; the number lives in a revision history table
on the right margin. `draw_revision_triangle` returns BOTH the triangle
handle and the inserted text handle so the agent can later move them
together as one logical mark.

## 7. Dimensioning style differs from architecture

Architectural dims default to linear with continuous extension lines.
Mechanical dims default to **aligned** (parallel to the dimensioned edge)
with arrows and unit-aware text. Use the `ISO-25` dim style (or the
office-specific `ME-25`) — `ensure_mechanical_dimstyle` creates it
idempotently. Mixing the architectural and mechanical dim styles in one
drawing breaks the drawing template and confuses downstream BOM extractors
that count distinct dim styles.

## 8. Section hatch direction encodes material

Convention (ISO 128-50):
- 45° hatch (ANSI31)        → cast iron / generic;
- 45° hatch w/ pitch 1 mm   → steel;
- crossed 45° + 135°        → bronze, brass;
- 30° + 60° dashes          → aluminium;
- horizontal lines          → glass;
- random rocks / dots       → soil / concrete.

`draw_section_hatch` takes a `material` enum and picks the right pattern;
agents who pass `pattern: "ANSI31"` directly bypass this — discourage that
in tool descriptions and prefer the enum. Material → pattern mapping lives
in `MechanicalPatterns.cs` (single source of truth).

## 9. Layer key (ISO mechanical, 11 layers — the office standard we ship)

| layer            | colour | linetype     | weight | content                           |
| ---------------- | ------ | ------------ | ------ | --------------------------------- |
| `ME-VISIBLE`     | 7      | Continuous   | 0.50   | visible feature edges             |
| `ME-HIDDEN`      | 8      | HIDDEN       | 0.25   | hidden (occluded) edges           |
| `ME-CENTER`      | 4      | CENTER       | 0.18   | centrelines, axes                 |
| `ME-DIMS`        | 2      | Continuous   | 0.18   | dimensions                        |
| `ME-TEXT`        | 2      | Continuous   | 0.18   | notes, labels                     |
| `ME-SECTION`     | 1      | PHANTOM      | 0.70   | section cutting plane             |
| `ME-HATCH`       | 8      | Continuous   | 0.18   | section hatching                  |
| `ME-THREAD`      | 8      | HIDDEN       | 0.25   | thread minor-Ø arcs / lines       |
| `ME-CONSTRUCTION`| 9      | Continuous   | 0.13   | construction (non-plottable)      |
| `ME-TITLE`       | 7      | Continuous   | 0.50   | title block geometry              |
| `ME-REV`         | 1      | Continuous   | 0.50   | revision triangles + tags         |

`ensure_mechanical_layers` creates this key idempotently. Every mechanical
draw tool calls it first.

## 10. Bundled blocks under `blocks/mechanical/`

Phase-6 v1 ships the geometry inline (no DWG dependency). Phase 7 ships:

- `BOLT_HEX_M*.dwg` (M6–M24, top + side variants);
- `WASHER_FLAT_M*.dwg`;
- `BEARING_RADIAL_*.dwg` (608, 6200, 6300 series);
- `SURFACE_FINISH_BASIC.dwg` (the √ check-mark with attribute slots);
- `WELD_SYMBOL_BASIC.dwg`.

Sanitise filenames before `Path.Combine` (rule 36 §12 carries over).

## 11. Cross-reference with validators

Every mechanical draw tool ships with a paired validator rule:
- `draw_centerline` → `mech.centerlines.must-be-dashed`,
  `mech.centerlines.on-me-center-layer`;
- `draw_hidden_edge` → `mech.hidden.must-be-dashed`,
  `mech.hidden.on-me-hidden-layer`;
- `draw_threaded_hole` → `mech.threads.minor-arc-not-full-circle`;
- `draw_section_cut_line` → `mech.section.thick-line-weight`.

When you add a draw tool, add or update the matching YAML under
`validators/mechanical/` (rule 35 §8).
